; ============================================================================
; SYMBOL-TABLE RESIDUE #1 (tail of block 0)
; Recovered from : SINTRAN/ND500/nd-500-mon/nd-500-mon-j04.prog
; Region         : file offsets 0x00014..0x001FF
; Generated by   : byte-level analysis; every claim below was read from the bytes.
; ============================================================================
;
; *** WHAT THIS IS -- READ THIS FIRST ***
;
; This is NOT part of the SINTRAN III ":PROG" file format. A :PROG file carries
; NO symbol table (BRF carries symbols; PROG does not). The bytes catalogued
; below are UNINITIALISED BUFFER RESIDUE: a fragment of the MON-DEBUG build's
; symbol table that was left in the writing tool's buffer and got flushed to
; disk inside padding areas of the file. Do not mistake this for a :PROG
; structural feature.
;
; Verified :PROG container layout of this file (253356 bytes / 126678 words):
;   0x00000..0x0000D  7-word big-endian header (first/last addresses, not lengths)
;   0x0000E..0x001FF  zero tail + RESIDUE  <-- symbol table #1 lives here
;   0x00200..0x200C9  BANK 1 (program) image, 65381 words, loads at address 0
;   0x200CA..0x201FF  inter-bank PADDING   <-- symbol table #2 lives here
;   0x20200..0x3DDAB  BANK 2 (data) image, 60886 words; ends exactly at EOF
;
; *** RECORD FORMAT -- PROVEN ***
;
; All values are BIG-ENDIAN (ND-100 native). Every record is word-aligned and
; every record begins at an EVEN file offset. Layout:
;
;   +0        attr   (1 byte)  record kind; determines the value length
;   +1        lenb   (1 byte)  bit7 = flag (meaning UNKNOWN); bits6-0 = name length
;   +2        name   (lenb&0x7f bytes of ASCII, A-Z 0-9 _)
;   +2+n      pad    (1 byte 0x00, present IF AND ONLY IF the name length is odd,
;                     i.e. the name is padded out to a whole number of words)
;   then      value  (VW(attr) words, big-endian)
;
;   VW(attr):  0x40 -> 3 words   0x41 -> 3 words   0x50 -> 3 words
;              0x60 -> 3 words   0x68 -> 2 words   0x70 -> 2 words
;              0x90 -> 1 word
;
; HOW THIS WAS PROVEN (no heuristics, no "scan for the next plausible byte"):
;   Parsing deterministically with the table above -- taking the value length
;   ONLY from the record's own attr byte -- consumes both regions with every
;   name coming out clean ASCII and the final record ending EXACTLY on the
;   region boundary (0x00200 and 0x20200 respectively). All 6^7 = 279936
;   possible attr->length mappings were enumerated by brute force; EXACTLY ONE
;   closes both chains. The encoding is therefore uniquely forced by the data.
;
; There is NO terminator byte. Each region simply runs out of room: the record
; stream is cut off by the start of the next real block (bank 1 at 0x200,
; bank 2 at 0x20200). The last record in each region ends exactly on that byte.
;
; *** VALUE ENCODING ***
;
; The value is W1[,W2[,W3]] big-endian words. W1 acts as a kind/type word.
; Observed W1: 0x0010 0x0080 0x0082 0x0083 0x00E0 0x0100 0x0110 0x8042
;
; PROVEN (by unambiguous name/value agreement, multiple independent cases):
;   For W1 low byte 0x83 or 0x82, W2 is the named constant's integer VALUE:
;     AENONE=0  AESINTRAN=1  AEOCTOBUS=2  AENUCLEUS=3   (enum, declaration order)
;     BMASTER=0 BSLAVE=1
;     PMMESSACK=0  PMMESSNAK=255 (0xFF)   (ACK/NAK)
;   The "Value" column below is emitted ONLY for these records.
;
; UNKNOWN (raw bytes given instead; NOT guessed):
;   - Meaning of bit7 (0x80) of the length byte. It is NOT a function of attr,
;     of name length, of name-length parity, or of W1. (One partial pattern:
;     within table #1's attr=0x40 records, flag=1 correlates perfectly with
;     W3=0x607C and flag=0 with W3=0x54E2 -- but this does NOT generalise to
;     table #2, so no rule is claimed.)
;   - Meaning of the attr byte itself beyond "it selects the value length".
;   - Meaning of W3. It repeats across related symbols (e.g. 0x1F90 shared by
;     all four ADDERRTYPE enum members), which is CONSISTENT with a type
;     reference -- but that is INFERENCE, not proof.
;   - Meaning of W1 bits, and of W2 for W1 low byte other than 0x82/0x83.
;   - Whether the CR* pair (W2,W3) forms one 32-bit quantity.
;
; *** THE CR* ADDRESS HYPOTHESIS -- TESTED AND REFUTED ***
;
; The eight CR* symbols carry a strictly increasing W3:
;   CRBXRDWORD 0x6B20  CRBRKDET 0x6B38  CRCACHEMOD 0x6B67  CRCSTEP  0x6B7F
;   CRDOWNLD   0x6B93  CRDTBFUNC 0x6BAA  CRECHOTEST 0x6BD1  CREVREP  0x6BFA
; They are NOT ND-100 word addresses of command routines in this binary:
;   Test 1 (disassembly). Bank 1 loads at address 0 from file 0x200, so word
;     address A -> file 0x200+2A. Disassembling each of the eight lands inside
;     valid ND-100 code but never at a routine entry: 0x6B38 = "JPL I -102"
;     (a call), 0x6B7F = "SUB 135", 0x6BFA = "JMP 23". Worse, 0x6B80..0x6B86
;     is plainly DATA -- an indirect-address table targeted by the JPL I
;     instructions around it -- so all eight fall INSIDE one single routine
;     spanning roughly 0x6B1C..0x6BFA. Eight distinct command routines cannot
;     live there.
;   Test 2 (reference search). NOT ONE of the eight values occurs anywhere in
;     bank 1 as a literal word, and no window of bank 1 or bank 2 contains even
;     4 of them together. A monitor command dispatcher would have to hold these
;     in a table. No such table exists.
;   Test 3 (bank 2). Read as bank-2 data addresses they land mid-string
;     (0x6B20 -> "cal segment: \ME"). Refuted.
; CONCLUSION: W3 of the CR* records is UNKNOWN. Because the residue's pointer-
; like words (0x61CE, 0xC9B5, 0x607C, 0x54E2, 0x1F90, 0x0B9F ...) are likewise
; referenced nowhere in either bank, the residue most likely came from a tool
; whose own arena these offsets describe, NOT from this file's banks -- but
; that is INFERENCE and is not proven here.
;
; *** OTHER PROVEN FACTS ***
;   - Maximum name length is 10 characters. Two names are visibly truncated to
;     exactly 10: CRCACHEMOD (from CRCACHEMODE) and TADDERRTYP (from
;     TADDERRTYPE, whose untruncated 10-char form ADDERRTYPE also appears).
;   - Records occur in a repeating 3-record group: CR<base> (attr 0x60),
;     T<base> (attr 0x68), T<base> (attr 0x70) -- the two T records of a group
;     carry identical names AND identical values, differing only in attr.
;   - Anonymous records exist: length byte 0x80 -> flag set, name length ZERO.
;     Two occur in table #1 after ETTESTPATT.
;
; *** TRUNCATED HEAD OF THIS REGION ***
; The region does not start on a record boundary. At 0x14 the bytes read
; "RINFO" followed by a well-formed 2-word value 00 10 61 CE, with the next
; record starting cleanly at 0x1E. Bytes 0x0E..0x13 are ZERO.
; FACT: the ASCII tail "RINFO" at 0x14..0x18 is the end of a clobbered name.
; INFERENCE (strong, but labelled as inference -- the bytes are zeroed and
; cannot be read back): the record was TBERRINFO with attr 0x68, because
;   (a) "TBERRINFO"[4:] == "RINFO" exactly;
;   (b) a 9-char name at 0x10..0x18 + pad at 0x19 + 2-word value at 0x1A..0x1D
;       + next record at 0x1E is the exact geometry required, and it fits to
;       the byte;
;   (c) its value 00 10 61 CE is exactly the T* 2-word value;
;   (d) the next record is TBERRINFO with attr 0x70, and every group in this
;       table is (CR* attr 0x60, T* attr 0x68, T* attr 0x70) -- so the missing
;       record is precisely the attr-0x68 TBERRINFO.
; It is listed below as index 0 with its recoverable bytes only.
;
